{"step_index":1675,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T00:00:54Z","thinking":"**Considering Roman Domination Number**\n\nThe goal is to prove an upper bound on the Roman domination number of a graph. Begin by applying the inequality and introducing an arbitrary Roman dominating function. Focus on the Roman weight of the function, and apply a case split on whether the cardinality of the vertex set is less than or equal to the function's weight. If the cardinality is small, the goal is immediately achieved.\n\n\n**Analyzing Cases with Radius Constraints**\n\nIf no vertices have a value of 2 under the function, an upper bound on the Roman domination number can be obtained. Otherwise, there must exist at least one vertex with a value of 2.  Consider the case when $\\text{radius}(G) \\ge 4$; if there are two vertices where the Roman dominating function equals 2, the Roman weight is at least 4. Consider the case when $\\text{radius}(G) \\ge 4$ and the Roman weight is less than four, this would be a contradiction.\n\n\n**Examining Distance in Subsets**\n\nConsidering $|V_2| = 2$, inferring the weight, $W = 4 + |V_1|$. Every vertex in $V_0$ must be within one hop of $V_2 = \\{u, u_2\\}$, implying all of $V_0$ lies within $B_1(u) \\cup B_1(u_2)$. Including $u$ and $u_2$, all vertices except possibly those in $V_1$ belong to $B_1(u) \\cup B_1(u_2)$.\n\n**Evaluating Radius with Connectedness**\n\nAnalyzing a connected graph where $V = B_1(u) \\cup B_1(u_2)$ to determine the possible radius.  If $u$ and $u_2$ are adjacent, $\\text{dist}(u, x) \\le 2$ for all $x$, giving radius at most 2. If $u$ and $u_2$ have a common neighbor, then for any $x \\in V$, the eccentricity is bounded, leading to $\\text{radius}(G) \\le 3$.\n\n\n**Bounding Radius by Examining Cases**\n\nIf $u$ and $u_2$ don't share a neighbor, and $V = B_1(u) \\cup B_1(u_2)$, a path exists from $u$ to $u_2$ with length at most 3, leading to $\\text{dist}(u,u_2) \\le 3$. This means $\\text{eccentricity}(x) \\le 3$ for some $x$, so the radius is at most 3. If $V = B_1(u) \\cup B_1(u_2)$, the radius is ALWAYS at most 3.\n\n**Verifying Radius with Roman Weight**\n\nWhen the Roman weight $W = 4$, and $|V_2| = 2$, it is deduced that $V_1 = \\emptyset$. This implies it is impossible for $\\text{radius}(G) \\ge 4$ if $W=4$. The set $V$ is a subset of $B_1(u)$.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean","StartLine":150,"toolAction":"View GraphInvariantsComputable.lean","toolSummary":"View Roman domination definition"}}]}
{"step_index":1676,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T00:01:02Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T02:01:02+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T02:01:02+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean`\nTotal Lines: 537\nTotal Bytes: 27292\nShowing lines 150 to 537\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n150: no `Decidable` instance, so `decide` cannot touch it — leaving `bipartite` a\n151: class that could be *stated* in an exported theorem but never refuted on a\n152: witness. Searching the powerset is decidable and finite; `isBipartiteClass_iff`\n153: proves the two agree. -/\n154: def IsBipartiteClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n155:   ∃ s ∈ (univ : Finset V).powerset,\n156:     ∀ u ∈ (univ : Finset V), ∀ v ∈ (univ : Finset V), G.Adj u v → (u ∈ s ↔ v ∉ s)\n157: \n158: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n159:     Decidable (IsBipartiteClass G) := by\n160:   unfold IsBipartiteClass; infer_instance\n161: \n162: /- ── vertex cover ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n163: \n164: /-- `s` covers every edge. This is mathlib's `SimpleGraph.IsVertexCover`\n165: restricted to a `Finset`, which is what makes it decidable: mathlib quantifies\n166: over `Set V` and its `vertexCoverNum` is an `⨅` over that, hence noncomputable.\n167: `isVertexCoverFinset_iff` below proves the two agree, so the mathlib definition\n168: remains the specification and nothing is taken on trust. -/\n169: def IsVertexCoverFinset (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (s : Finset V) :\n170:     Prop :=\n171:   ∀ u ∈ (univ : Finset V), ∀ v ∈ (univ : Finset V), G.Adj u v → u ∈ s ∨ v ∈ s\n172: \n173: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (s : Finset V) :\n174:     Decidable (IsVertexCoverFinset G s) := by\n175:   unfold IsVertexCoverFinset; infer_instance\n176: \n177: theorem isVertexCoverFinset_iff (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n178:     (s : Finset V) : IsVertexCoverFinset G s ↔ G.IsVertexCover (↑s : Set V) := by\n179:   simp [IsVertexCoverFinset, SimpleGraph.IsVertexCover]\n180: \n181: def vertexCoverNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n182:   minCard (IsVertexCoverFinset G)\n183: \n184: /- ── zero-forcing family ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n185: \n186: def forcingStep (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) : Finset V :=\n187:   S ∪ univ.filter (fun w => w ∉ S ∧ ∃ v ∈ S, G.neighborFinset v \\ S = {w})\n188: \n189: def forcingClosure (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) : Finset V :=\n190:   (forcingStep G)^[Fintype.card V] S\n191: \n192: def IsZeroForcingSet (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) : Prop :=\n193:   forcingClosure G S = univ\n194: \n195: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) :\n196:     Decidable (IsZeroForcingSet G S) := by\n197:   unfold IsZeroForcingSet; infer_instance\n198: \n199: def zeroForcingNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n200:   minCard (IsZeroForcingSet G)\n201: \n202: def IsTotalZeroForcingSet (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) : Prop :=\n203:   IsZeroForcingSet G S ∧ ∀ v ∈ S, ∃ w ∈ S, G.Adj v w\n204: \n205: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) :\n206:     Decidable (IsTotalZeroForcingSet G S) := by\n207:   unfold IsTotalZeroForcingSet; infer_instance\n208: \n209: def totalZeroForcingNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n210:   minCard (IsTotalZeroForcingSet G)\n211: \n212: /-- `totalZeroForcingNumber` is meaningful: a total zero-forcing set exists.\n213: False whenever some vertex has no neighbour in any forcing set — isolated\n214: vertices being the common case. -/\n215: def HasTotalZeroForcingNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n216:   HasWitness (IsTotalZeroForcingSet G)\n217: \n218: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n219:     Decidable (HasTotalZeroForcingNumber G) := by\n220:   unfold HasTotalZeroForcingNumber; infer_instance\n221: \n222: /-- One step of reachability inside `S`: everything already in `T`, plus the\n223: vertices of `S` adjacent to it. -/\n224: def reachWithin (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S T : Finset V) : Finset V :=\n225:   T ∪ S.filter (fun w => ∃ v ∈ T, G.Adj v w)\n226: \n227: /-- The vertices of `S` reachable from `v` without leaving `S`. Iterating `n`\n228: times suffices: each step that changes anything adds at least one vertex. -/\n229: def componentWithin (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) (v : V) :\n230:     Finset V :=\n231:   (reachWithin G S)^[Fintype.card V] {v}\n232: \n233: /-- `S` induces a connected subgraph.\n234: \n235: The specification layer says `(G.induce ↑S).Connected`, which is a statement\n236: about walks in a subtype and carries no `Decidable` instance. This is the\n237: decidable equivalent: `S` is nonempty and every vertex of it reaches all of `S`\n238: from inside. `Connected` is `Preconnected ∧ Nonempty`, so both conjuncts are\n239: accounted for — the nonemptiness is not incidental. -/\n240: def IsConnectedSubset (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) : Prop :=\n241:   S.Nonempty ∧ ∀ v ∈ S, componentWithin G S v = S\n242: \n243: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) :\n244:     Decidable (IsConnectedSubset G S) := by\n245:   unfold IsConnectedSubset; infer_instance\n246: \n247: /-- A connected zero-forcing set: zero-forcing and inducing a connected\n248: subgraph, matching graphcalc's `is_connected_zero_forcing_set`. -/\n249: def IsConnectedZeroForcingSet (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) :\n250:     Prop :=\n251:   IsZeroForcingSet G S ∧ IsConnectedSubset G S\n252: \n253: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (S : Finset V) :\n254:     Decidable (IsConnectedZeroForcingSet G S) := by\n255:   unfold IsConnectedZeroForcingSet; infer_instance\n256: \n257: def connectedZeroForcingNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n258:   minCard (IsConnectedZeroForcingSet G)\n259: \n260: /-- `connectedZeroForcingNumber` is meaningful: a connected zero-forcing set\n261: exists. False on every disconnected graph, which is precisely where graphcalc\n262: reports no value and where the conjectures never made a claim. -/\n263: def HasConnectedZeroForcingNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n264:   HasWitness (IsConnectedZeroForcingSet G)\n265: \n266: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n267:     Decidable (HasConnectedZeroForcingNumber G) := by\n268:   unfold HasConnectedZeroForcingNumber; infer_instance\n269: \n270: /- ── graph-class predicates ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n271: \n272: def IsNontrivialClass (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop := 2 ≤ order G\n273: \n274: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) : Decidable (IsNontrivialClass G) := by\n275:   unfold IsNontrivialClass; infer_instance\n276: \n277: def IsRegularClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n278:   ∀ u v : V, G.degree u = G.degree v\n279: \n280: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n281:     Decidable (IsRegularClass G) := by unfold IsRegularClass; infer_instance\n282: \n283: def IsCubicClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n284:   ∀ v : V, G.degree v = 3\n285: \n286: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n287:     Decidable (IsCubicClass G) := by unfold IsCubicClass; infer_instance\n288: \n289: def IsSubcubicClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n290:   ∀ v : V, G.degree v ≤ 3\n291: \n292: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n293:     Decidable (IsSubcubicClass G) := by unfold IsSubcubicClass; infer_instance\n294: \n295: /-- Eulerian: connected, with every degree even (graphcalc's `eulerian` is\n296: `networkx.is_eulerian`, which requires connectivity — see GraphInvariants). -/\n297: def IsEulerianClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n298:   G.Connected ∧ ∀ v : V, G.degree v % 2 = 0\n299: \n300: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n301:     Decidable (IsEulerianClass G) := by unfold IsEulerianClass; infer_instance\n302: \n303: /-- Triangle-free: no 3-element clique. -/\n304: def IsTriangleFreeClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n305:   ∀ s ∈ univ.powerset, IsCliqueFinset G s → s.card ≠ 3\n306: \n307: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n308:     Decidable (IsTriangleFreeClass G) := by\n309:   unfold IsTriangleFreeClass; infer_instance\n310: \n311: /-- `K₄`-free: no 4-element clique. -/\n312: def IsK4FreeClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n313:   ∀ s ∈ univ.powerset, IsCliqueFinset G s → s.card ≠ 4\n314: \n315: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n316:     Decidable (IsK4FreeClass G) := by unfold IsK4FreeClass; infer_instance\n317: \n318: /-- Connected. Mathlib's `SimpleGraph.Connected` already has a `Decidable`\n319: instance on a finite vertex type (`Connectivity/Finite.lean`) and it *does*\n320: reduce in the kernel — verified directly — so this is a plain alias rather than\n321: a reimplementation. -/\n322: def IsConnectedClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop := G.Connected\n323: \n324: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n325:     Decidable (IsConnectedClass G) := by unfold IsConnectedClass; infer_instance\n326: \n327: /-- A tree: connected with `m + 1 = n`.\n328: \n329: Mathlib's `SimpleGraph.IsTree` is built on `IsAcyclic`, a statement about *all*\n330: walks, and has no `Decidable` instance — `decide` fails on it outright. The edge\n331: count is the decidable equivalent, and `isTreeClass_iff` below proves the two\n332: agree, so nothing is taken on trust. -/\n333: def IsTreeClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n334:   G.Connected ∧ size G + 1 = order G\n335: \n336: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n337:     Decidable (IsTreeClass G) := by unfold IsTreeClass; infer_instance\n338: \n339: omit [DecidableEq V] in\n340: /-- The computable tree test is exactly mathlib's `IsTree`. -/\n341: theorem isTreeClass_iff (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n342:     IsTreeClass G ↔ G.IsTree := by\n343:   rw [SimpleGraph.isTree_iff_connected_and_card]\n344:   simp [IsTreeClass, size, order, Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, Set.toFinset_card,\n345:         SimpleGraph.edgeFinset]\n346: \n347: /-- Claw-free: no vertex has three pairwise non-adjacent neighbours.\n348: \n349: Phrased through `IsIndependentFinset` over `powersetCard 3` rather than as four\n350: plain `∀ v a b c : V` binders with `a ≠ b` side conditions: the latter is the\n351: more readable statement (and is what `GraphInvariants.lean` records) but its\n352: `Decidable` instance does not reduce, so `decide` gets stuck on it. This form\n353: reduces. -/\n354: def IsClawFreeClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n355:   ∀ v ∈ (univ : Finset V), ∀ s ∈ univ.powersetCard 3,\n356:     (∀ u ∈ s, G.Adj v u) → ¬ IsIndependentFinset G s\n357: \n358: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n359:     Decidable (IsClawFreeClass G) := by unfold IsClawFreeClass; infer_instance\n360: \n361: /-- Cograph: no four vertices induce a path `P₄`. -/\n362: def IsCographClass (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n363:   ∀ a b c d : V, G.Adj a b → G.Adj b c → G.Adj c d →\n364:     ¬ G.Adj a c → ¬ G.Adj b d → G.Adj a d\n365: \n366: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n367:     Decidable (IsCographClass G) := by unfold IsCographClass; infer_instance\n368: \n369: /- ── sub-total domination ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n370: \n371: /-- Sub-total domination number: least `t` whose `t` largest degrees sum to\n372: `≥ n`.\n373: \n374: graphcalc computes this by sorting the degree sequence and scanning prefixes;\n375: `largestDegreeSum` is that prefix sum expressed as a maximum over `t`-subsets\n376: (see the note there on why sorting is not available to the kernel). -/\n377: def subTotalDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n378:   minUpTo (Fintype.card V) (fun t => Fintype.card V ≤ largestDegreeSum G t)\n379: \n380: /-- Defined exactly when *some* prefix reaches `n`. graphcalc returns no value\n381: when the scan runs off the end — an edgeless graph has every degree `0`, so no\n382: prefix ever reaches `n` — while `minUpTo` would fall back to `n`. Monotonicity\n383: of `largestDegreeSum` makes the whole-graph check `t = n` sufficient. -/\n384: def HasSubTotalDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n385:   Fintype.card V ≤ largestDegreeSum G (Fintype.card V)\n386: \n387: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n388:     Decidable (HasSubTotalDominationNumber G) := by\n389:   unfold HasSubTotalDominationNumber; infer_instance\n390: \n391: /- ── total domination ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n392: \n393: /-- Every vertex has a neighbour in `s` — note `G.Adj v u` excludes `v = u`, so\n394: a vertex may not dominate itself. -/\n395: def IsTotalDominatingSet (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (s : Finset V) :\n396:     Prop := ∀ v : V, ∃ u ∈ s, G.Adj v u\n397: \n398: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (s : Finset V) :\n399:     Decidable (IsTotalDominatingSet G s) := by\n400:   unfold IsTotalDominatingSet; infer_instance\n401: \n402: def totalDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n403:   minCard (IsTotalDominatingSet G)\n404: \n405: /-- Undefined on a graph with an isolated vertex: nothing dominates it, so not\n406: even `univ` is a total dominating set and `minCard` would read `0`. graphcalc\n407: raises there rather than returning a number. -/\n408: def HasTotalDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n409:   HasWitness (IsTotalDominatingSet G)\n410: \n411: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n412:     Decidable (HasTotalDominationNumber G) := by\n413:   unfold HasTotalDominationNumber; infer_instance\n414: \n415: /- ── edge cover ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n416: \n417: /-- A set of edges meeting every vertex. -/\n418: def IsEdgeCoverFinset (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n419:     (s : Finset (Sym2 V)) : Prop := ∀ v : V, ∃ e ∈ s, v ∈ e\n420: \n421: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (s : Finset (Sym2 V)) :\n422:     Decidable (IsEdgeCoverFinset G s) := by\n423:   unfold IsEdgeCoverFinset; infer_instance\n424: \n425: /-- Edge cover number: least `k ≤ n` such that some `k` edges meet every vertex.\n426: \n427: Searched by size over `edgeFinset`, not over its whole powerset. A minimum edge\n428: cover never needs more than `n - 1` edges (Gallai: `ρ = n − ν`, and `ν ≥ 1` once\n429: there is an edge), so bounding `k` by `n` loses nothing. It matters: the full\n430: powerset of `K₆`'s 15 edges is 32,768 subsets and exhausted the kernel's\n431: recursion depth, while the subsets of size at most 6 number 9,949. -/\n432: def edgeCoverNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n433:   minUpTo (Fintype.card V)\n434:     (fun k => ∃ s ∈ G.edgeFinset.powersetCard k, IsEdgeCoverFinset G s)\n435: \n436: /-- Undefined exactly when some vertex is isolated — networkx raises \"no edge\n437: cover exists\" there, and `minUpTo`'s fallback would read `n`. An edge cover\n438: exists iff every vertex has a neighbour (take one incident edge per vertex), so\n439: this is stated directly instead of by a second subset search. -/\n440: def HasEdgeCover (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n441:   ∀ v : V, ∃ u : V, G.Adj v u\n442: \n443: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n444:     Decidable (HasEdgeCover G) := by unfold HasEdgeCover; infer_instance\n445: \n446: /- ── Roman domination ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n447: \n448: /-- `f : V → {0,1,2}` is a Roman dominating function when every vertex labelled\n449: `0` has a neighbour labelled `2`.\n450: \n451: graphcalc solves this as a MIP over binaries `x, y` with `f = x + 2y`,\n452: constraint `x v + y v + Σ_{u ∈ N(v)} y u ≥ 1` and exclusion `x v + y v ≤ 1`;\n453: that is exactly the condition below. -/\n454: def IsRomanDominatingFunction (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n455:     (f : V → Fin 3) : Prop := ∀ v : V, f v = 0 → ∃ u : V, G.Adj v u ∧ f u = 2\n456: \n457: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (f : V → Fin 3) :\n458:     Decidable (IsRomanDominatingFunction G f) := by\n459:   unfold IsRomanDominatingFunction; infer_instance\n460: \n461: def romanWeight (f : V → Fin 3) : ℕ := ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ)\n462: \n463: /-- Total, not partial: the constant function `2` is always Roman dominating\n464: (no vertex is labelled `0`), so a witness always exists. -/\n465: def romanDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n466:   (((univ.filter (IsRomanDominatingFunction G)).image romanWeight).min).getD 0\n467: \n468: /- ── double Roman domination ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n469: \n470: /-- `f : V → {0,1,2,3}` is double Roman dominating when a vertex labelled `0`\n471: has either a neighbour labelled `3` or two neighbours labelled `2`, and a vertex\n472: labelled `1` has a neighbour labelled `≥ 2`.\n473: \n474: The first clause is written as graphcalc's linearisation\n475: `0.5·|N₂(v)| + |N₃(v)| ≥ 1`, but as two existentials rather than as\n476: `Finset.card` of a filter: \"at least two neighbours labelled 2\" is \"two distinct\n477: neighbours labelled 2\", and the kernel reduces a pair of quantifiers over `V`\n478: far more cheaply than it builds and counts a `Finset`. Written the counting way\n479: first, all seven order-5 checks exhausted their heartbeat budget. -/\n480: def IsDoubleRomanDominatingFunction (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n481:     (f : V → Fin 4) : Prop :=\n482:   (∀ v : V, f v = 0 →\n483:       (∃ u : V, G.Adj v u ∧ f u = 3) ∨\n484:       (∃ u : V, ∃ w : V, u ≠ w ∧ G.Adj v u ∧ G.Adj v w ∧ f u = 2 ∧ f w = 2)) ∧\n485:   (∀ v : V, f v = 1 → ∃ u : V, G.Adj v u ∧ 2 ≤ (f u : ℕ))\n486: \n487: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (f : V → Fin 4) :\n488:     Decidable (IsDoubleRomanDominatingFunction G f) := by\n489:   unfold IsDoubleRomanDominatingFunction; infer_instance\n490: \n491: def doubleRomanWeight (f : V → Fin 4) : ℕ := ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ)\n492: \n493: /-- Total, for the same reason as `romanDominationNumber`: constant `3` works. -/\n494: def doubleRomanDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n495:   (((univ.filter (IsDoubleRomanDominatingFunction G)).image\n496:     doubleRomanWeight).min).getD 0\n497: \n498: /- ── radius ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n499: \n500: /-- Vertices within `k` steps of `v`, by iterated neighbourhood expansion.\n501: Distance is not available to the kernel directly (`SimpleGraph.dist` is defined\n502: through `sInf` over walk lengths), so it is reconstructed as reachability. -/\n503: def ballWithin (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ → V → Finset V\n504:   | 0,       v => {v}\n505:   | (k + 1), v =>\n506:       let B := ballWithin G k v\n507:       B ∪ B.biUnion (fun u => univ.filter (fun w => G.Adj u w))\n508: \n509: /-- Eccentricity of `v`: least `k` with every vertex within `k` steps. -/\n510: def eccentricity (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V) : ℕ :=\n511:   minUpTo (Fintype.card V) (fun k => ballWithin G k v = univ)\n512: \n513: def radius (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n514:   ((univ.image (eccentricity G)).min).getD 0\n515: \n516: /-- Undefined on a disconnected graph, where some vertex never reaches all of\n517: `univ` and `minUpTo` would fall back to `n`. networkx raises there. -/\n518: def HasRadius (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n519:   ∀ v : V, ballWithin G (Fintype.card V) v = univ\n520: \n521: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n522:     Decidable (HasRadius G) := by unfold HasRadius; infer_instance\n523: \n524: /- ── graph construction from an explicit edge list ────────────────────────── -/\n525: \n526: /-- The graph on `Fin n` with the given edge list, symmetrised and loop-free.\n527: This is the shape the disproof exporter emits for a refuting witness.\n528: \n529: `SimpleGraph.fromRel` supplies the symmetry and irreflexivity proofs, so this\n530: stays a definition rather than a bundle of obligations that would have to be\n531: re-discharged (and re-adjusted across mathlib versions) in generated code. It is\n532: an `abbrev` so that `DecidableRel` synthesis can see through it at the use site. -/\n533: abbrev ofEdges (n : ℕ) (es : List (Fin n × Fin n)) : SimpleGraph (Fin n) :=\n534:   SimpleGraph.fromRel (fun u v => (u, v) ∈ es)\n535: \n536: end GraphCalc\n537: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
